{"context":"eka-steward hands a bounded deterministic context payload (internal/render) to a coding agent and drives one prompt turn, over the Agent Client Protocol (ACP), without becoming a second EKA write path and without spawning subprocesses outside the audited escape hatch. ACP v2 is a breaking revision; the agents installed on the target machines (opencode 2.4.1, gemini 0.42.0) negotiate protocolVersion 1 only, and opencode implements several v2-named session methods under v1 shapes. Spike evidence: docs/acp-spike.md (sto:acp-client-spike).","decision":"1) Version: ship ACP v1 (protocolVersion 1) now behind a per-connection version dispatcher; send the highest version supported and act on the returned version. Do not request 2 until a v2 handler exists, because a v2 request against a v1 agent silently drops clientCapabilities (losing embeddedContext). Treat v2 as additive per v2_migration.md.\n2) Method set (v1 now): initialize; session/new; session/prompt; consume session/update; session/cancel; answer session/request_permission; optional session/load|session/resume for reattach; optional session/set_config_option; session/close. Under v2: session/load -\u003e session/resume + replayFrom; session/set_mode -\u003e session/set_config_option; completion via idle state_update.\n3) Structured context: deliver the internal/render payload in session/prompt as a resource block when the agent advertises embeddedContext, else a text block; keep the task instruction as a separate text block. No systemPrompt.\n4) Permission policy: deny by default; allow read-class tool calls only inside the session workspace root; escalate execute/edit/delete/move to an explicit decision; never auto-select allow_always; bound every permission wait (default 120 s) and answer cancelled on expiry or session/cancel (v2: also $/cancel_request) so a worker never hangs; never treat an unknown optionId/outcome as approval.\n5) Subprocess boundary: the stdio spawn stays in the audited internal/escapehatch family; a streaming-stdio adapter is added there (or a sibling audited package), never an os/exec import elsewhere.","consequences":"Ships against the agents developers actually have; no v2 dead-end. The 8 KiB render budget fits one resource block; the handoff was verified end-to-end against opencode. v2 adoption is a handler swap, not a rewrite, but requires the new prompt lifecycle (state_update), message IDs, upsert tool calls and $/cancel_request before flipping the advertised version. Default-deny bounded permissions trade some autonomy for safety; operators can widen the allow-list. opencode's ACP mode currently auto-approves tools, so the steward's own command policy and workspace-root checks remain load-bearing. escapehatch.Run captures whole output today; a streaming pipe is a new small audited surface to add before the client lands.","alternativesConsidered":"MCP as the driver (rejected in M7: the plugin/proxy host is deadline-bounded and minimal-env, hostile to a long-running orchestrator; eka-cli stays the escape hatch). Speak ACP v2 only (rejected: no v2 agent on the target machines, and a v2 request against a v1 agent silently drops capabilities). Grant allow_always on the first read (rejected: unbounded autonomy past the workspace root)."}